    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Relevant Theorem: Order of Operations The Order of Operations is a set of rules that dictate how to evaluate mathematical expressions involving multiple operations.\n\nLooking at the expression $1+6\\cdot2-3+5\\cdot4\\div2$, we can see that there are both multiplication and division operations and addition and subtraction operations. Therefore, we must follow the Order of Operations to determine the correct order in which to perform these operations.\n\nAccording to the Order of Operations, we should perform multiplication and division operations before addition and subtraction operations. In addition, according to the Order of Operations, we should perform operations from left to right.\n\nLet's use the PEMDAS mnemonic to help us remember the Order of Operations:\n\n1. Parentheses\n2. Exponents\n3. Multiplication and Division (from left to right)\n4. Addition and Subtraction (from left to right)\n\nNow, let's evaluate the expression step by step:\n\n1. There are no parentheses, so we move on to\n\n$1+6\\cdot2-3+5\\cdot4\\div2$\n(Both $\\cdot$ and $\\div$ are multiplication and division operations, they should be performed from left to right)\n$1+6\\cdot2-3+5\\cdot\\cancel{4}\\div2$\n(There is nothing left to either\n\nEvaluate the following proper fraction.\n\n$\\mathrm{(A)\\ } \\frac15 \\qquad \\mathrm{(B) \\ }\\frac12 \\qquad \\mathrm{(C) \\  } \\frac23 \\qquad \\mathrm{(D) \\  } \\frac34 \\qquad \\mathrm{(E) \\  }\\frac45$",
