    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the expression \\(1 + 6 \\cdot 2 - 3 + 5 \\cdot 4 \\div 2\\), we need to follow the order of operations, often remembered by the acronym PEMDAS (Parentheses, Exponents, Multiplication and Division (from left to right), Addition and Subtraction (from left to right)).\n\nFirst, we perform the multiplication and division operations from left to right:\n\n1. Calculate \\(6 \\cdot 2\\):\n   \\[\n   6 \\cdot 2 = 12\n   \\]\n   So the expression now is:\n   \\[\n   1 + 12 - 3 + 5 \\cdot 4 \\div 2\n   \\]\n\n2. Next, calculate \\(5 \\cdot 4\\):\n   \\[\n   5 \\cdot 4 = 20\n   \\]\n   So the expression now is:\n   \\[\n   1 + 12 - 3 + 20 \\div 2\n   \\]\n\n3. Then, perform the division \\(20 \\div 2\\):\n   \\[\n   20 \\div 2 = 10\n   \\]\n   So the expression now is:\n   \\[\n   1 + 12 - 3 + 10\n   \\]\n\n4. Finally, perform the addition and subtraction from left to right:\n   \\[\n   1 + 12 = 13\n   \\]\n   So the expression now is:\n   \\[\n   13 - 3 + 10\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   13 - 3 = 10\n   \\]\n   So the expression now is:\n   \\[\n   10 + 10\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   10 + 10 = 20\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the expression \\(1 + 6 \\cdot 2 - 3 + 5 \\cdot 4 \\div 2\\) is \\(\\boxed{20}\\).",
